\section{Introduction}\label{sec:history-intro}

The history of programming languages is the history of human attempts to bridge the gap between human thought and machine execution. Each language represents a set of design decisions---about abstraction, about safety, about performance, about the nature of computation itself. Understanding this history is essential for the language designer: it reveals which ideas have endured, which have been abandoned, and why.

This chapter traces the evolution of programming languages from machine code through the great language families of the modern era. We organize the history around three major lineages---the systems programming lineage, the functional lineage, and the object-oriented lineage---and examine the cross-pollination that has produced modern languages like Rust, Go, Swift, and Kotlin.

\section{From Machine Code to High-Level Languages}\label{sec:machine-to-high-level}

\subsection{The First Generation: Machine Language}

The earliest ``programming'' was done in raw machine language: binary or hexadecimal codes representing processor instructions. Programming was an exercise in patience and precision---every instruction had to be hand-coded, every memory address manually computed.

\begin{historical}
The first programs were written for the Manchester Baby (1948) and the EDSAC (1949). These programs were entered by setting switches on the front panel or by punching paper tape. There was no assembler, no compiler, no abstraction---just the bare metal.
\end{historical}

\subsection{The Second Generation: Assembly Language}

Assembly language replaced numeric codes with mnemonic names (\texttt{ADD}, \texttt{MOV}, \texttt{JMP}) and symbolic labels for memory addresses. The \emph{assembler} translated assembly into machine code. This was the first major abstraction: programmers could think in terms of operations and labels rather than binary codes and numeric addresses.

Key innovations of assembly language:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Symbolic names}: Instructions and data could be referred to by name rather than number.
  \item \textbf{Macros}: Reusable sequences of instructions could be defined and expanded.
  \item \textbf{Conditional assembly}: Code could be included or excluded based on conditions evaluated at assembly time.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Transition to High-Level Languages}

The leap from assembly to high-level languages was motivated by several factors:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Portability}: Assembly language is machine-specific. A high-level language could be compiled for different machines.
  \item \textbf{Productivity}: High-level languages express algorithms in terms closer to human thought.
  \item \textbf{Maintainability}: High-level code is easier to read, understand, and modify.
  \item \textbf{Mathematical rigor}: High-level languages could be given formal semantics, enabling reasoning about programs.
\end{enumerate}

\section{The Pioneers: Fortran, Lisp, COBOL, ALGOL}\label{sec:pioneers}

\subsection{Fortran (1957)}

\emph{Fortran} (Formula Translation), developed by John Backus and his team at IBM, was the first widely used high-level programming language~\cite{backus1978can}.

\begin{description}
  \item[Design goals:] Efficient compilation of mathematical formulas; competitive performance with hand-coded assembly.
  \item[Key innovations:] The first optimizing compiler; variables with types; array subscript notation; \texttt{DO} loops; subroutines and functions.
  \item[Legacy:] Fortran dominated scientific computing for decades. Modern Fortran (Fortran 2008 and later) includes modules, object-oriented features, and coarrays for parallel computing.
\end{description}

\begin{historical}
Backus's 1978 Turing Award lecture, ``Can Programming Be Liberated from the von Neumann Style?,'' argued that Fortran's von Neumann style (sequences of assignments to mutable variables) was a fundamental barrier to productive programming. He advocated for \emph{functional programming} as an alternative---a vision that would take decades to realize.
\end{historical}

\subsection{Lisp (1958)}

\emph{Lisp} (List Processing), created by John McCarthy at MIT, was the second high-level language and the first to be based on the lambda calculus~\cite{mccarthy1960recursive}.

\begin{description}
  \item[Design goals:] Symbolic computation; list processing; recursive function definitions.
  \item[Key innovations:] The lambda calculus as a computational model; garbage collection; homoiconicity (code as data); the read-eval-print loop (REPL); conditional expressions; recursive functions.
  \item[Legacy:] Lisp spawned an enormous family of languages: Scheme, Common Lisp, Clojure, Racket, and many dialects. Its ideas (garbage collection, REPLs, macros, higher-order functions) have been adopted by nearly every modern language.
\end{description}

\subsection{COBOL (1959)}

\emph{COBOL} (Common Business-Oriented Language) was designed for business data processing.

\begin{description}
  \item[Design goals:] English-like syntax; record processing; decimal arithmetic; portability across business computers.
  \item[Key innovations:] Record structures; file handling; decimal arithmetic; self-documenting syntax (verbose but readable).
  \item[Legacy:] COBOL became the dominant language for business applications and remained in widespread use for decades. As of 2020, an estimated 200 billion lines of COBOL code were still in production, running on mainframes in banks, insurance companies, and government agencies.
\end{description}

\subsection{ALGOL (1958--1968)}

\emph{ALGOL} (Algorithmic Language) was the most influential language in the history of programming language design. Though never widely used in industry, it introduced concepts that became standard in virtually every subsequent language.

\begin{description}
  \item[Design goals:] A universal notation for describing algorithms; a language suitable for publication in scientific journals.
  \item[Key innovations:] Block structure (lexical scoping); nested function definitions; \texttt{begin}/\texttt{end} blocks; \texttt{for} loops; Backus--Naur Form (BNF) for syntax description; call-by-name parameter passing; the first formal semantics (via BNF).
  \item[Legacy:] ALGOL 60 and ALGOL 68 directly influenced Pascal, C, Ada, and most subsequent imperative languages. BNF became the standard notation for grammar description.
\end{description}

